#import "../lib.typ": *

#section(
  exercise([
    #drill-heading(125)

    As explained in a former lesson, capital Q is simply a large figure two.
    It is a good movement drill. Practice it as such. In all your word
    practice a special effort should be made to space the letters evenly.
    Study your written lines with that in mind.
  ], lines: 6, slots: 2, examples: ("drill-125", "drill-125-2")),
  exercise([
    #drill-heading(126)

    Pupils should give particular attention to the appearance of their
    finished pages, making letters and words conform to spaces. The capitals
    should occupy only from two-thirds to three-fourths the distance between
    the ruled lines, assuming that they are about three-eighths of an inch
    apart.

    From fifteen to twenty capitals should be made to a line. If the forms are
    well made, a page of capitals written in accordance with these suggestions
    will present a very pleasing appearance.

    The crossing of capital F in this drill is above the center, and the final
    stroke at the crossing is small. In practice, write the full word every
    time the capital is made. Do not let your practice work approach
    scribbling. Do the very best you can, not part of the time, but all the
    time.
  ], lines: 6, examples: ("drill-126", "drill-126-2")),
  exercise([
    #drill-heading(127)

    Always start capital I with an upward stroke from below the base line. The
    upper part should be about one-half the width of the lower. The crossing
    of the beginning with the main downward stroke should be one space above
    the base line. Practice the detached capital as well as the word until
    there is an improvement in the movement and its application.
  ], lines: 6, slots: 2, examples: ("drill-127", "drill-127-2")),
  exercise([
    #drill-heading(128)

    Practice and compare, and then practice again. Capital J is twice as wide
    above as below the base, and the lower part is a little shorter than the
    upper part. Pupils who find the letter troublesome should review lessons
    forty-nine and fifty.
  ], lines: 6, examples: ("drill-128", "drill-128-2")),
  exercise([
    #drill-heading(129)

    Write a page of capital E's and another page of the words.
  ], lines: 6, slots: 2, examples: ("drill-129", "drill-129-2")),
  exercise([
    #drill-heading(130)

    Capital B was discussed at considerable length in lesson seventy-one, and
    students who have failed to master the letter should turn to that lesson
    for review.
  ], lines: 6, slots: 2, examples: ("drill-130", "drill-130-2")),
)
